         <h3>Thirteen more translations</h3>
         <p>Thomas Hobbes, the philosopher of <em>Leviathan</em>, published his <em>Odyssey</em> in 1675, in his eighties. Anne Dacier, thought to be the first woman to translate Homer, spent some fifteen years putting the <em>Iliad</em> and the <em>Odyssey</em> into French prose; her <em>Odyssey</em>, of 1716, joins Leconte de Lisle&rsquo;s. William Morris, the Arts and Crafts designer, wrote much of his on socialist speaking tours, in deliberately archaic English. Worsley, Palmer and Cotterill bring the new English translations to five.</p>
